1.3 Fonts that are NOT redistributed
    The desktop shell's CJK fallback reads faces already installed on the
    host (`oxigis-desktop/src/font_scan.rs`) and the web shell fetches its
    CJK and print-only faces at runtime (`oxigis-web/src/font_fetch.rs`).
    Neither path embeds anything, so neither adds an obligation here — but a
    redistributor who chooses to bundle those fetched faces takes on their
    licences and must extend this file accordingly.


================================================================================
2. Third-party Rust dependencies
================================================================================

`Cargo.lock` is the authoritative list of the several hundred crates in the
resolved dependency graph. Their licences are constrained by `deny.toml`'s
`[licenses] allow` list, which admits only permissive terms — Apache-2.0,
Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception, MIT, MIT-0,
BSD-2-Clause, BSD-3-Clause, ISC, Zlib, Unicode-3.0, MPL-2.0, CC0-1.0,
CDLA-Permissive-2.0, 0BSD, Unlicense, BSL-1.0, and the font licences above —
and `cargo deny check licenses` fails on anything else.

A per-crate attribution inventory (THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.md) is NOT yet
generated and is a prerequisite for the first public binary release, because
several Apache-2.0 dependencies carry NOTICE files of their own that section
4(d) requires propagating. Generate it with `cargo about generate` or
`cargo deny check licenses` output and commit it beside this file.


================================================================================
3. Known gap: this file does not reach the published crates
================================================================================

Cargo packages a crate from its own directory and never walks above it, so
none of the five `crates/oxigis-*` tarballs contain this file — verify with
`cargo package --list -p oxigis-web`, which lists neither NOTICE nor LICENSE.

The obligations in section 1 attach to each distributed artifact, so before
the first `cargo publish` each shipping crate needs its own copy (a
`crates/<crate>/NOTICE` alongside a `crates/<crate>/LICENSE`, or a
`license-file` pointer in its manifest), and the desktop/wasm release archives
need this file beside the binary. Until then, redistribution is compliant only
from this repository, not from crates.io.
